      "question": {
        ":question/id": "b988807a-34eb-52af-995a-9e3c8a1f0e2f",
        ":question/problem": "The point $(-7,8)$ lies on the curve $y=2f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-4?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-7,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-4:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=2f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (8))=(-7,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-(4))=(-7,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $4$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-4-4)=(-7,-8).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-7,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=2f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n2f(-7)=8\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-7)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ Substituting $x=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-7)-4\\\\&=-(4)-4\\\\&=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-7,-8)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-4.$",
