      "question": {
        ":question/id": "b55e01e5-9c24-510f-825f-e89bf23a1ddf",
        ":question/problem": "The point $(1,2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-4?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(1,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-4:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,3\\cdot (2))=(1,6).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-(6))=(1,-6).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $4$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-6-4)=(1,-10).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(1)=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(1)=6.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(1)-4\\\\&=-(6)-4\\\\&=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,-10)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-4.$",
